\chapter{Ideas for future RefTeX support}
% November 8, 2025

My development plan is to add support for \noweb{}'s macros to \reftex and
\auctex then use the information gleaned from these packages to
buttonize and markup (with overlays) the chunk headers and
cross-references in poly-noweb buffers. \texttt{diff}s of the
continually watched product of the \noweb{}-formatted source file and the
user-modified \LaTeX{}-formatted document (which is a derivation of the
former), and suggestions for filters could conceivably allow two-way
communication between \noweb{} and \auctex. Would that be \whyse{}? I don't
think it would be wise to call it \whyse{}---the \textsc{WHS} implemented
in \emacs{}---but it would still be \whyse{}: \emph{a particular}
\textsc{((no)Web~HYpertext~System~in~Emacs)}. It may still be a stepping
stone to the original notion, which would be interesting to explore, but
I think it would be better than that original goal as well; it wouldn't
be as flexible as the \noweb{} system is, however, as it would only
support \LaTeX{} documentation, whereas \noweb{} supports other backends.

\begin{quote}
  \reftex has been bundled and pre-installed with \emacs{} since version
  20.2.

  --- the \reftex \texinfo manual, ``with typographic editions''.
\end{quote}

Given \reftex has been a part of \emacs{} for so long it makes sense to
simply defer to its featureset where it provides something useful,
rather than replacing it with new functionality in Emacs; I haven't
surpassed the featureset or implementation of \reftex, certainly not, so
rather than replacing that I'll add to it.

The following diagram shows which lines, in a Noweb document, need
RefTeX support and what kind. Within a chunk there are cross-references
