\chapter{Event-hook Loop}\label{event-hook-loop}
There has always been some confusion in my mind as to how the system
will work. Reading a noweb file is no issue, neither is parsing the tool
syntax of that file, but when these operations should take place in the
editing cycle has been elusive.

Implementing the \textsc{WHS} using the UNIX toolbox philosophy---many small
tools chained together to create solutions and systems---honours the
noweb architecture itself, but I won't be calling [[shell-command]] too
much. The original inspiration when I began writing WHYSE's literate
program was correct: I need to gather from the user a [[shell-command]]
to work with the project sources as they are. Users may be adopting
\whyse after already working on their projects for some time, and with
some filters and other advanced modes of operation in play (for
instance, not using the default shell wrappers to call the C backends or
the Icon filters).

My own document currently builds with a simple Make rule, but I do use
options! I'm not simply calling [[noweave]].

\begin{verbatim}
whyse.tex: autodefs.elisp | builddir
	cd $(BUILD) && $(NOWEB_BIN)/noweave -delay -autodefs elisp -index $(SRC)/whyse.nw > $@
\end{verbatim}

